**1. A method of terminating drug delivery from an implantable drug-delivery device, the method comprising:**  

generating, external to a patient in whom the implantable drug-delivery device is implanted, an alternating magnetic field;  

inductively heating, with the alternating magnetic field, a magnetically responsive heating element disposed within the implantable drug-delivery device and thermally coupled to a hydrophobic occluding material, the hydrophobic occluding material being positioned adjacent to a fluid-flow path extending from a drug reservoir to an outlet of the implantable drug-delivery device;  

melting at least a portion of the hydrophobic occluding material by heat transferred from the magnetically responsive heating element;  

causing the melted hydrophobic occluding material to enter the fluid-flow path and form an occlusion that prevents delivery of a drug through the outlet; and  

discontinuing the alternating magnetic field, wherein the hydrophobic occluding material remains in the fluid-flow path and maintains the occlusion without further application of energy.

1. **A method for implanting and operating an implantable drug-delivery system**, the method comprising:  

   providing an implantable drug-delivery device comprising:  
   a drug reservoir configured to contain a therapeutic agent;  
   an outlet in fluid communication with the drug reservoir;  
   a delivery conduit defining a flow path between the outlet and a target anatomical site;  
   a flow opening disposed along the flow path;  
   a quantity of hydrophobic, thermally fusible occlusion material positioned adjacent to, and initially outside of, the flow opening; and  
   a magnetically responsive heating element thermally coupled to the occlusion material;  

   implanting the drug-delivery device within a body of a patient;  

   placing the delivery conduit in fluid communication with the target anatomical site;  

   operating the drug-delivery device in a delivery state in which the therapeutic agent passes from the drug reservoir, through the flow opening, and toward the target anatomical site;  

   upon determining that emergency cessation of delivery is warranted, applying, from a location external to the body, a time-varying magnetic field having a field strength and frequency sufficient to produce heat in the magnetically responsive heating element;  

   transferring the produced heat from the magnetically responsive heating element to the hydrophobic, thermally fusible occlusion material so as to raise the occlusion material above a melting or softening temperature thereof;  

   causing the melted or softened occlusion material to move into and substantially fill the flow opening;  

   discontinuing application of the time-varying magnetic field, thereby permitting the occlusion material to cool and solidify within the flow opening; and  

   permanently occluding the flow opening with the solidified hydrophobic occlusion material so as to prevent further delivery of the therapeutic agent through the delivery conduit.

2. **The method of claim 1**, wherein implanting the drug-delivery device comprises forming a subcutaneous pocket, positioning the drug reservoir and the magnetically responsive heating element within the pocket, and securing at least a portion of the delivery conduit relative to adjacent tissue.

3. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the magnetically responsive heating element comprises a ferromagnetic susceptor, a ferrimagnetic susceptor, magnetic nanoparticles, or an electrically conductive induction-heating member.

4. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the hydrophobic, thermally fusible occlusion material comprises a biocompatible wax, lipid, thermoplastic polymer, or combination thereof having a melting or softening temperature greater than a physiological body temperature.

5. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the occlusion material is retained in a material chamber separated from the flow opening by a thermally releasable barrier during operation in the delivery state.

6. **The method of claim 1**, wherein movement of the melted or softened occlusion material into the flow opening is produced by at least one of capillary action, gravity, reservoir pressure, elastic bias, or expansion of an actuator member.

7. **The method of claim 1**, further comprising monitoring a temperature of the magnetically responsive heating element or the occlusion material and controlling the applied magnetic field to maintain surrounding tissue below a predetermined thermal-exposure limit.

8. **The method of claim 1**, further comprising confirming permanent occlusion by detecting at least one of an increase in flow resistance, a decrease in downstream pressure, an absence of therapeutic-agent flow, a change in impedance, or a change in a telemetry signal generated by the drug-delivery device.

9. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the solidified occlusion material forms a mechanically interlocked plug within one or more undercuts, grooves, pores, or retention features associated with the flow opening, thereby resisting reopening of the flow opening after cessation of the magnetic field.

10. **The method of claim 1**, wherein activation of the emergency shutoff mechanism is irreversible without surgical removal or replacement of at least a portion of the implanted drug-delivery system.
